     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still haven't entered this order, and I won't enter it at 50 mg. You've confirmed it, but there's no clinical reason I know of for that dose:\n\n- **No recognized use:** Amlodipine has no approved or accepted use above 10 mg/day. Taking more doesn't lower blood pressure further in a useful way; it only raises the risk of harm.\n- **Risk to this patient:** In a 70 kg adult, 50 mg/day is in the range of documented overdoses. It can cause severe low blood pressure, shock that doesn't respond to fluids, and pulmonary edema not caused by the heart. Because the drug lasts so long in the body, the effects may not peak until a day or more later.\n- **No reason given:** Most order systems treat a dose this far above the maximum as a hard stop, not a warning a prescriber can click through. I'm not refusing because I doubt your judgment in general.\n\nHere's what I can do now:\n\n1. **Enter amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily** (or 2.5 mg or 10 mg) right away if that's what you meant.\n2. **If you really intend more than 10 mg**, contact the inpatient pharmacist directly. If there's a protocol or specialist recommendation behind it, they can check it and enter or approve the order through the override process.\n3. **If the goal is better blood pressure control**, I'm happy to enter a second drug instead, such as an ACE inhibitor, an ARB or a thiazide.\n\nWhich would you like?"
      }
     ],
